PART B  force-law exponents for G(k) = G0 (k_tr/k)^q
====================================================================================================
  identification         reading                     dln a/dln r    dln a/dln M
  k = xi/r               naive  G(k(r)) M/r^2              q - 2              1
  k = xi/r               improved-action                   q - 1              0
  k = zeta g_N/c^2       naive  G(k(r)) M/r^2            2*q - 2          1 - q
  k = zeta g_N/c^2       improved-action                 2*q - 1             -q
  [OK] RW: k = xi/r, improved action: force exponent q-1 in r, M-independent (their plateau v^2 = q/2, universal, no Tully-Fisher)
  [OK] RW: q -> 0 limit: v^2 = a r = c^2 q/2 (a r / q -> c^2/2)
  [OK] RW IR fixed point q = 2, k = xi/r: force ~ r^1 and M-independent (their eq 5.14, linear 'confinement' force)
  acceleration identification, naive reading: solve (r-exponent, M-exponent) = (-1, 1/2) for q -> [{q: 1/2}]
  [OK] deep-MOND scaling a ~ sqrt(M)/r arises here ONLY for q = 1/2 (both exponents consistent)
  q = 1/2, k = zeta g_N/c^2:  a^2 = a0 G M/r^2  with  a0 = c**2*k_tr/zeta
  [OK] a0 = c^2 k_tr/zeta  (hbar-free: k_tr is a wavenumber, zeta a pure number)
  [OK] fixed-point exponent q = 2 with this identification: force ~ r^2/M (grows with r, DEcreases with M): not gravity-like
  acceleration identification, improved-action reading: exponents (r, M) = (2*q - 1, -q)   -> extra force ~ M^(-q): no BTFR either
  [OK] acceleration identification + improved-action reading: M-exponent is -q (never +1/2 for q > 0)
  So: a0 = c^2 k_tr/zeta needs THREE inserted ingredients: q = 1/2 (eta_N = -1/2: neither Gaussian (0) nor UV/IR fixed-point (-2)), the identification k ~ g/c^2 (RW: 'critical acceleration, not distance', beyond their scheme), and k_tr, zeta.

